  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery with 500mb heights plotted shows the upper \nridge axis shifting east and weakening as it does, and a \nshortwave trough lifting northeastward out of Mexico and \nsouthwestern Texas. As that shortwave moves into Oklahoma and \nKansas tonight into tomorrow, forcing associated with it will \nlead to isolated to scattered showers, and possibly a few \nthunderstorms, across eastern/ northeastern Kansas and \nnorthwestern Missouri. Instability is very weak to nonexistent \nacross the area so the chances for any storms is quite low. So \nthe general expectation is for be just showers. This system will\nalso lead to a brief cool down (though still above normal) in \ntemperatures Tuesday. \n\nTemperatures rebound Wednesday through Friday as persistent \nsoutherly flow allows temperatures to climb back into the upper 70s \nto potentially near 80 degrees. With normal highs in the lower 60s \nfor the Kansas City area, we should be 15 to as much as 20 degrees \nabove normal. There is some uncertainty regarding this, however. A \nfrontal boundary may slide south through the area leading to \nincreased precipitation chances and cooler (though still above \nnormal) temperatures. For now, the most probable scenario is for \nprecipitation to occur across northern Missouri, in the vicinity of \nthe boundary, with areas south of that boundary to climb one again \ninto the upper 70s to near 80 degrees. A similar scenario plays out \nFriday, with the most likely areas to see precipitation across \nnorthern Missouri/ northeastern Kansas, and south of that climb into \nthe upper 70s. If precipitation occurs further south, that would \nlimit just how warm much of the forecast area gets. That's a \npossibility (~<25% of occurrence), just not as probable as what's \nalready been mentioned. For Saturday, the best chances for \nprecipitation look earlier than in previous forecasts as the \nshortwave moves over the area earlier. This results in decreasing \nPoPs by the early evening hours across eastern Kansas and western \nMissouri. While temperatures trend cooler for the weekend, we may \nstill be 10 degrees above normal Saturday - Monday.",
